Stage 11: the core-ffi arm on M2. A NON-LEAF element, encode and decode.
          Plus the decomposition that says which half of it costs what.

commit:    ba83970b (+ this slice)
core:      ffi/poc/codec/target/release/libak_core.so, the ONE core (R0), default
           features, REBUILT after merging the exploration branch. 86 ak_ exports
           and 800,104 bytes, against the 66 and 618,264 stage 9 recorded: the
           core grew, and no tonic or tokio is linked in (nm -D | grep -ci tokio
           = 0), so ABI v1 section 9's RPC half is still behind its feature.
           A SECOND build with --features count produced the crossing table below
           and was then swapped back out; every timing is the default build.
           **M1's figures were re-taken against this core** and moved a little;
           both readings are given where they differ.
binding:   generated, ffi/poc/csharp/gen/cs_abi.py + cs_coreffi.py + cs_coreffi2.py
linkage:   shared library, P/Invoke via LibraryImport, reverse calls via
           [UnmanagedCallersOnly] with the abort guard
strings:   STAGED as UTF-8 with tc = ak_tc_bytes(), a function pointer INTO the
           core, so the binding makes zero transcoder crossings
R13:       the rust crossing benchmark measures 1.8 ns forward and 2.1 ns
           forward-plus-reverse on this container
           (ffi/logs/csharp/calibration-rust-crossing.log). This slice's own
           measurement of what a crossing costs .NET is 7.5 to 12 ns
           (stage10-crossing-reconciliation.log), and that is the figure to use
           below, not the rust one.

WHY M2 IS A DIFFERENT MEASUREMENT FROM M1, not a bigger one.

  ResultRaw is a LEAF. Its encode vtable is empty, the whole run crosses once,
  and the crossing count is CONSTANT in the element count: 2 forward + 1 reverse
  for a thousand-element encode, 1 forward + 2 reverse back. That is ABI v1
  section 6's batching predicate doing the thing it exists for.

  TaskDetailed is not a leaf. It carries four repeated string fields and a map,
  so the predicate does not hold and the codec calls back once per loop slot PER
  ELEMENT. Decode is section 7.2's refusal in the interface: there would be
  nothing to attach an inner element to, so it is new_tasks then apply_tasks per
  element plus one run per inner field that occurred.

  The published C# claim this stage exists to test is ".NET's composed arm beats
  its own managed codec on DECODE" -- 0.899 on M1/P1.2. Until now that was a
  claim about one flat leaf message.

WHAT THE ABI SURFACE COST BEFORE ANYTHING WAS MEASURED. Declaring M2 caught one
defect: a vtable slot invented by analogy. ak_dvt_TaskDetailed has an
unk_options_options slot; ak_dvt_ListTasksDetailedResponse does not, and
declaring one would have shifted every later slot by a pointer and called a
garbage address. Vtable SLOT COUNTS are now asserted beside struct sizes and
offsets. The presence bit values are read from the Rust constants through
gen/abi-layout.json rather than inferred from a field's position among the
singular message children -- the obvious rule is right today and is still a
guess, and it is the same class of guess as the invented slot, in the same file.

################ correctness gate, against a counting core ################

Layout agreement (ABI v1 obligation 12.3, as far as it goes here):
  ok: 20 structs and 5 vtables match the Rust build; core ak_abi_version()=1

M2 through the C ABI. Encode is byte identity against the validated
manifest; decode is a re-encode to the same bytes AND a field-by-field
comparison, because a re-encode alone passes a decoder that drops a field
the encoder also omits -- and on M2 the map is exactly such a field.

  chunk = the whole run elements per ak_elemu_* call

