The columns are the HOST's tally. The core's own ak_enc_counters read the same numbers, checked above and equal on every row

Reading the crossing columns. THE TWO CONVENTIONS ARE THE SAME QUANTITY.
  Where they used to disagree it was a CHUNK SIZE and not a convention:
  set AK_CHUNK to what the other host chunks at and the columns match to
  the digit. The core's `reverse` additionally counts transcoder calls, and
  this binding stages its strings so it makes none. See JOURNAL.md 19.

  ENCODE, per element, with every loop slot populated:
    4 repeated string fields + 1 map = 5 reverse calls, and the host calls
    forward again for each run that is not empty, so about 10 crossings a
    task. Constant in the element count only in the sense that it is
    LINEAR in it, which is exactly what the batching predicate buys on M1
    and cannot buy here.
  DECODE: ABI v1 section 7.2 refuses to batch a non-leaf, so it is
    new_tasks then apply_tasks per element plus one run per inner field
    that occurred: 2 + 5 = 7 reverse calls a task against ONE for a whole
    thousand-element M1 response.

0 failure(s)

R5 IS CHECKED HERE, NOT ASSERTED. That gate ran against a --features count core
and compared the HOST's own tally with the core's ak_enc_counters and
ak_dec_counters, per payload and per direction. They are EQUAL on every row, and
a mismatch is counted as a failure rather than printed as a note.

That settles by measurement something this slice once got wrong in prose. THE TWO
CROSSING COLUMNS ARE THE SAME QUANTITY and always were. The only thing the core
counts that a host tally cannot see is a transcoder invocation, and this binding
stages its strings so it makes none. The gap once reported against the rust slice
was a CHUNK SIZE (JOURNAL.md 19): their host hands the codec 150 elements per
element call, this one hands over the whole run. The same gate with AK_CHUNK=150:

payload  elements    bytes  encode  decode  value   fwd   rev   fwd    rev   fwd   rev   fwd    rev
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
P2.1            1     1037  ok      ok      ok        7     6  7.00   6.00     1     8  1.00   8.00
P2.2          500   540422  ok      ok      ok     2505  2501  5.01   5.00     1  3501  0.00   7.00
P2.3          125   647024  ok      ok      ok      627   626  5.02   5.01     1   876  0.01   7.01
P2.4           80   979465  ok      ok      ok      402   401  5.03   5.01     1   561  0.01   7.01
P2.5           20    19632  ok      ok      ok      102   101  5.10   5.05     1   141  0.05   7.05

Chunking at 150 adds 3 forward calls to a 500-element encode -- ceil(500/150) = 4
ak_elemu_* calls instead of 1 -- and changes nothing else. R5 still matches.

THE CROSSING COUNTS MATCH THE RUST SLICE TO THE DIGIT.

  encode  5 forward + 5 reverse per element = 10.00/task on P2.2.
          The rust slice measures 10.02/task.
  decode  7 reverse per element = 7.00/task on P2.2, plus one forward for the
          whole response. The rust slice measures 7.004/task.

  On encode the root is still constant -- loop_tasks is ONE reverse call for the
  whole response and ak_elemu_TaskDetailed is one forward call per chunk. Inside
  each element the codec calls back once per loop slot (four repeated string
  fields and the map, five) and the host calls forward again for each run that is
  not empty, ak_blob_run for the strings and ak_elem_TaskOptionsOptionsEntry for
  the map. Ten. On decode, new_tasks and apply_tasks per element is two, plus one
  run per inner field, five. Seven, against ONE add_results for a whole
  thousand-element M1 response.

  P2.1 reads 7.00/6.00 and 1.00/8.00 per element because it has ONE element and
  the root's own calls are not amortised. P2.5 reads 5.10/5.05 and 7.05 because
  it is the half-absent payload and some runs are empty.

