DECODE, P2.2, three runs, because the first one disagreed with the other harness
and one run is not an answer:
run 1: gp-parse-seq 2.112 ms  managed-parse 1.772 ms  core-ffi 1.594 ms -> 0.900
run 2: gp-parse-seq 2.258 ms  managed-parse 1.713 ms  core-ffi 1.650 ms -> 0.963
run 3: gp-parse-seq 2.113 ms  managed-parse 1.707 ms  core-ffi 1.685 ms -> 0.987

  BenchmarkDotNet's own spread on that ratio is 0.900 to 0.987; the interleaved
  harness's is 0.923 to 1.052. THEY OVERLAP. The first BDN run's 0.900 is exactly
  M1/P1.2's published figure, and taking it as the answer would have confirmed
  the published claim from the bottom of a spread. That is why it was re-run.

  One reproducible structural difference, identical in all three BDN runs: the
  two decode arms allocate the SAME 2.02 MB per operation and build the same
  graph, but Gen1 collections per 1,000 operations are 85.9 for core-ffi against
  119.1 for the managed control, with Gen0 identical at 121.1. Fewer promotions
  for the same bytes. Recorded, not chased: it is deterministic, and a GC
  difference moves with the heap and should not be quoted as a time.

################ what this says ################

1. **DECODE: THE PUBLISHED CLAIM DOES NOT SURVIVE M2, AND IT DOES NOT REVERSE.**
   ".NET's composed arm beats its own managed codec on decode" was 0.899 on
   M1/P1.2 and re-reads 0.863 against the current core. On M2 it is 0.97 to 1.14
   across the five payloads, with every spread touching or crossing 1.0 and the
   two harnesses overlapping each other on P2.2 at 0.90 to 1.05. The honest
   statement is that ON A NON-LEAF ELEMENT THE COMPOSED ARM AND THE PURE MANAGED
   CODEC ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE ON DECODE, and the M1 margin is gone.

   Both remain well under the incumbent -- on the M2 rows core-ffi is 0.76 to
   0.92 of gp-parse-seq and managed-parse is 0.76 to 0.89 -- so the story
   against Google.Protobuf is unchanged. What changed is the story against the
   design's own control.

   The mechanism is section 7.2's refusal: seven reverse calls per element
   against one for a whole M1 response, each carrying a GCHandle resolve and a
   castclass because [UnmanagedCallersOnly] cannot capture and there is no
   cheaper way to reach the target graph from a native pointer.

2. **ENCODE: THE INTERFACE COST RISES, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE CROSSINGS.**
   M1/P1.2 is 1.250 against the current core; M2 is 1.69 to 1.94. But ten
   crossings a task at this slice's own measured .NET price of 7.5 to 12 ns is 75
   to 120 ns, and the gap between core-ffi and managed on P2.2 is
   (850,812 - 501,511)/500 = 699 ns a task. The crossings are 11 to 17 percent
   of it and the fill-only arm says where the rest is.

   THE HOST-SIDE GROUP FILL IS 40 TO 57 PERCENT OF THE WHOLE CORE-FFI ENCODE, on
   every payload of both shapes, in both harnesses. On P2.2 the fill alone is
   383 us against a managed encode of 502 us: **the fill by itself is 76 percent
   of the entire managed codec**, and it more than accounts for the interface
   cost. Subtract it and the core's own work plus every crossing is 0.93 of the
   managed control on P2.2, 0.74 on P1.2, and below 1.0 on six of eight payloads.

   **So on encode the Rust codec across a C ABI is FASTER than the C# codec, and
   the arm loses anyway because of what the host must do to feed it.** That is
   ABI v1 decision 9 -- the sparse fill -- and this is the measurement that says
   it is the amendment worth making, not a nicety.

3. **P1.3 WAS MISREAD, AND THE FILL ARM SAYS SO.** stage 8 recorded the absent
   path collapsing to 2.361 and attributed it to the group fill by elimination.
   Elimination was right about the cause and wrong about the scope. The fill is
   62.6 percent of P1.3's encode (BDN: 64.2), but it is also 40 percent of
   P1.1's and P1.2's, where nothing is absent. THE COST IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE
   ABSENT PAYLOAD. It is a property of the group being filled whole, and P1.3
   only makes it visible by having nothing else in the encode to hide behind.

4. **THE PULL FAMILY IS NOW THE ARM TO BUILD, for a reason this slice supplies
   rather than borrows.** design/ABI-v1.md decision 2 says pull REMOVES the
   upcalls rather than reducing them, and that four of five slices have only
   measured push. This slice's M1 decode is one upcall per response and beats the
   managed control; its M2 decode is 7.00 upcalls per element and ties it. That
   is the same correlation the java slice reports at 7.004 upcalls, from an
   independent binding on a different runtime. The codec already emits a pull
   family (dec_*_pull in the generated codec). Nobody has bound one from a
   managed host.

################ what is NOT measured here ################

- M3 to M7 through the C ABI. M3 is where the oneof and explicit presence live,
  and it is the next binding rather than a measurement of this one.
- The pull decode family (ABI v1 7.1), which paragraph 4 says is the arm to
  build. Not built in any managed slice.
- An RPC arm. The core's rpc feature is off in the object this stage loaded.
- Arms b and c. net48 has no LibraryImport and no UnmanagedCallersOnly, so the
  core-ffi arm does not exist on the floor runtime at all (stage9).
- A sparse-fill arm. Paragraph 2 prices the cost of NOT having one; it does not
  measure a version that has one, because the ABI does not offer it yet.
- The host-transcoder string form. Still predicted, still not measured.
- Whether the Gen1 difference in the BDN decode table survives a server GC or a
  different heap. Recorded and left.

